<section class="band-white"><div class="container-page min-w-0 space-y-12">
<Section id="interpretation" index="01" eyebrow="Interpretation" title="Muller v King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust" citation="[2017] EWHC 128 (QB), [2017] QB 987">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: perceptual error in image interpretation.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: missed malignant melanoma on foot imaging with later expert review showing a visible lesion.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: Bolam applies to judgment. It does not protect failure to see what is plainly visible to a competent radiologist. Peer evidence that others might also have missed it does not cure a perceptual miss of a demonstrable abnormality.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Muller is the most-cited modern authority for the proposition that Bolam governs <em>judgment</em> but not <em>perception</em>. It is the case to put alongside Penney (index 02) whenever a missed lesion was visible in retrospect on archived images.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> the defence that "many would have missed it" fails if the lesion was demonstrably there. Protect the report instead: systematic search, comparison with priors, and honest expression of uncertainty where the study is limited.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> Muller defeats the "pure diagnosis" shortcut in the claimant's favour too — visibility must be proved as fact, with the original images, before Bolam drops away. Instruct the expert to address demonstrability first.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="screening" index="02" eyebrow="Screening" title="Penney, Palmer and Cannon v East Kent HA" citation="[1999] Lloyd's Rep Med 123; aff'd [2000] Lloyd's Rep Med 41 (CA)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: standard of care in screening imaging and cytology — when <em>Bolam</em> does not apply. Commonly cited as <em>Penny v East Kent Health Authority</em>.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: three false-negative cervical screening reports. Cytopathologists and screeners classified slides showing precancerous cells as negative. The judge found the abnormalities were plainly visible on the slides.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: first question is factual — did the slide show abnormalities. If present, failure to report or refer fell below standard. Evidence that others might have missed it was not determinative. <em>Buchan, Clinical Negligence: A Practical Guide</em> (8th ed, Ch 6) treats this as the leading example of the Bolam / Bolitho boundary: where there is no genuine exercise of skill and judgment to defend — a perceptual miss of what is demonstrably there — Bolam does not protect the defendant. The Court of Appeal affirmed. The same analysis applies in radiology to a plainly visible lesion missed on a radiograph or slide: see <em>Muller</em> (index 01) and <em>EXP v Barker</em> (index 07). UK and Scots law alike: the Scottish root of Bolam is <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em>, applied uniformly across both jurisdictions.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> screening and high-volume plain-film work live or die on perceptual vigilance. The case separates <em>misses</em> (nothing to defend under Bolam) from <em>judgment calls</em> (defensible). Double reading, checklists and protected reporting time directly answer this risk.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> if the lesion was there to see, peer evidence that others might have missed it will not save the report. Record what was reviewed, compare with priors, and state uncertainty rather than issuing a flat negative where doubt exists.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> plead and prove the factual premise first — was the abnormality demonstrably present on the archived images? Sequence expert evidence accordingly: visibility before opinion. Penney is the authority to cite when the defence runs Bolam against a plain perceptual miss.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="task-standard" index="03" eyebrow="Task standard" title="FB v Rana" citation="[2017] EWCA Civ 334 (claim against Princess Alexandra Hospital NHS Trust)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: standard for junior staff deciding whether to order — and how to interpret — diagnostic imaging.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: a deteriorating child with atypical symptoms. The junior doctor failed to take an adequate history and failed to order indicated brain and chest imaging, delaying diagnosis and causing permanent brain damage.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: standard is set by post and task, not by seniority or personal inexperience. A junior occupying an ED post who interprets a radiograph — or decides whether imaging is indicated — is judged against a reasonably competent practitioner doing that task. Failure to order imaging the post required was breach. <em>Buchan</em> (Ch 6) confirms: inexperience is no defence. RCR Standards (3rd ed, October 2025 — <em>ppqi_reporting-standards-guidance.pdf</em> extract) reinforce the corollary: non-radiology doctors and non-medical practitioners working beyond their imaging competence must have an agreement for radiology review, and no employee should work beyond competence or without adequate rest. Same rule in Scotland: <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em> states the standard by reference to the ordinary competent practitioner in the relevant role.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> most out-of-hours plain films are first read by non-radiologists. FB is the authority that holds that first read to the standard of the post, so ED and referral pathways must define who may issue a provisional read and when a formal radiology review is mandatory.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> you own the safety net — agree written review arrangements for examinations reported outside radiology, audit provisional reads, and refuse service models that leave juniors interpreting beyond competence without cover.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> do not discount a claim because the reader was junior. Frame breach against the occupant-of-the-post standard and test the Trust's supervision and review system alongside the individual miss.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="communication" index="04" eyebrow="Communication" title="Gwyn v South Tyneside and Sunderland NHS Foundation Trust" citation="[2021] (unreported — no neutral citation; verify before reliance)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Fail-safe reporting of critical findings.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/gwyn-2021-critical-findings-communication" class="link-strong">Read the full Gwyn case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="consent" index="05" eyebrow="Consent" title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board" citation="[2015] UKSC 11, [2015] AC 1430">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: informed consent for imaging and interventional procedures.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: material delivery risks not disclosed.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: disclosure judged by reasonable patient standard. For embolisation, biopsy and angioplasty, discuss material risks, complications and alternatives specific to that patient. Record the discussion.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Montgomery governs every image-guided procedure — biopsy, embolisation, angioplasty, injection — because the Montgomery duty attaches to the decision to intervene, and in radiology that decision is often the radiologist's. The MDU's Achilles-tendon case (damages £12,500, costs near five times that) shows undocumented verbal warnings settle claims even where the procedure itself was reasonable.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> consent is a documented dialogue, not a form: record the material risks discussed (including a ~1:1,000 rupture risk where relevant), the alternatives including doing nothing, and what this particular patient weighed. IR(ME)R justification and Montgomery disclosure should be recorded together.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> Montgomery claims turn on the consent record and on what a reasonable person in <em>this</em> patient's position would have wanted to know. Absence of documentation is routinely treated as absence of warning — subpoena clinic letters, lists and ultrasound report text, not just the signed form.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="incidental-findings" index="06" eyebrow="Incidental findings" title="Halligan et al. — Incidental Pathology in Abdominal Radiology Claims" citation="2025–2026 literature">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: what standard applies when the allegation concerns pathology incidental to the primary clinical question?</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: a retrospective review of 112 medicolegal cases by five expert abdominal radiologists (Halligan, Blunt, Boone, Higginson, Plumb) published in <em>Clinical Radiology</em> 92 (2026) 107162. Of 176 imaging examinations reviewed (56% CT, 20% MRI), 41 (37%) involved allegations citing failure to report incidental pathology. In 29 of these 41 cases (71%), the experts judged the interpretation as reasonable — not substandard. In the remaining 12 cases (29%), the interpretation was judged substandard. Across the entire cohort, the most common allegation was "primary tumour unreported" (40 cases, 36%), followed by "perforation unreported" (12 cases, 11%).</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: claimants routinely cite the RCR Standards for Interpretation and Reporting to support allegations that incidental pathology should have been detected. However, the authors — all acting as expert witnesses for both claimant and defendant — found that the Bolam test remains the proper framework. Where clinical details direct attention to a specific question (e.g. "abdominal pain ? cause" on an unprepared abdominal CT), failure to report a small incidental colonic tumour was often judged reasonable. The RCR 2025 Standards' recognition that "interpretation may be incorrect if referral details are deficient, absent or misleading" provides some protective recognition of this reality. The case is cited both as evidence of claimant strategy and as expert consensus that the incidentaloma question requires nuanced Bolam application rather than a "pure diagnosis" approach.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Halligan quantifies the incidentaloma litigation burden — 37% of abdominal claims — and rebuts the assumption that every missed incidental is negligence: 71% of challenged incidental interpretations were judged reasonable. It is the empirical backbone for defending inconspicuous misses made without a clinical steer.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> the protective factors are concrete — adequate referral information, attention-directing clinical details, and documented search of the relevant anatomy. Where referrals are vague ("abdominal pain ? cause"), say what the study can and cannot exclude, and push back on referral quality through governance channels.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> use Halligan to calibrate settlement — most incidental allegations fail on breach — but check which cohort the case falls in: missed tumour forming 56% of incidental allegations faces different expert dynamics from a small incidental lesion on an unprepared study. The RCR Standards citation cuts both ways and must be read with the 2025 referral-quality wording.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="missed-aneurysm" index="07" eyebrow="Missed aneurysm" title="EXP v Barker" citation="[2017] EWCA Civ 63">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: standard of care for diagnostic interpretation under the Bolam test and the weighting of expert witness testimony.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: a consultant neuroradiologist interpreted an MRI for severe headaches and missed a right middle cerebral artery aneurysm; twelve years later it ruptured, leaving permanent catastrophic brain damage.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: a radiologist is accountable where a reasonably competent specialist ought to have detected the abnormality, even if a defence expert argues the error was reasonable. The Court of Appeal upheld the High Court finding of negligence.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> aneurysm on MRA/MRI is the paradigm "demonstrable but missed" lesion — small, adjacent to vessel, and fatal if ruptured. EXP shows that a single missed aneurysm can sustain a catastrophic claim even against a consultant neuroradiologist.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> vascular review needs its own pass — source images as well as reconstructions, documented aneurysm search, and a low threshold for second opinion or addendum where headache imaging is equivocal.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> EXP pairs naturally with Muller and Penney: where the aneurysm is demonstrable on the archived study, defence expert evidence of reasonableness carries little weight. Fix the visibility fact early, then address limitation — here twelve years elapsed before rupture.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="misinterpretation" index="08" eyebrow="Misinterpretation" title="Brady v Southend University Hospital NHS Foundation Trust" citation="[2020] EWHC 289 (QB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Whether an incorrect diagnostic interpretation on imaging automatically constitutes negligence.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/brady-2020-diagnostic-interpretation" class="link-strong">Read the full Brady case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="subjective-variance" index="09" eyebrow="Subjective variance" title="Spellman v Portsmouth Hospitals University NHS Trust" citation="[2024] EWHC 2011 (KB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Standard of interpretation in a &quot;pure diagnosis&quot; radiology claim — when disagreement between radiologists is negligence and when it is not.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/spellman-2024-radiological-opinion" class="link-strong">Read the full Spellman case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="subtle-ct-signs" index="10" eyebrow="Subtle CT signs" title="White v Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust" citation="[2011] EWHC 3822 (QB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: diagnostic threshold for subtle visceral injury on CT imaging — what a competent consultant radiologist must spot.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: an abdominal CT on a trauma patient was reported as normal, missing early signs of bowel perforation (subtle extraluminal gas) that led to peritonitis and severe sepsis.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: judgment for the claimant. Expert evidence established that while subtle, the signs of extraluminal gas were present on the study and should have been identified by a reasonably competent consultant radiologist. This is the <em>Penney</em> side of the line (index 02): a factual miss of a demonstrable abnormality, not a defensible difference of opinion as in <em>Brady</em> (index 08) or <em>Spellman</em> (index 09). MPS analysis (<em>MPS medicolegal.txt</em> extract: highest reporting-error payment above £400,000, 2016–mid-2023) lists missed anastomotic leak and bowel perforation among the recurring reporting-error allegations. Practical standard: windowing, multiplanar review and explicit comment on extraluminal gas in trauma CT, per RCR reporting standards.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> trauma CT is reported fast, often overnight, where subtle extraluminal gas is the classic "present but missed" finding. White makes lung-window and multiplanar review of bowel and mesentery a litigation-tested standard, not a preference.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> build the negative into the report — state the windows and planes reviewed and that no extraluminal gas or leak was identified. If the study is degraded or the patient unstable, say so and advise what follow-up closes the gap.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> White is a visibility case masquerading as subtlety — obtain windowed, multiplanar reconstructions for the expert, and test whether the "subtle" sign was in fact demonstrable to a competent consultant at the time.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="whole-image" index="11" eyebrow="Whole-image duty" title="KJY v University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust" citation="[2023] EWHC 2719 (KB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Duty to examine the &quot;whole picture&quot; on a plain radiograph — including incidental pathology outside the referral question.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/kjy-2023-whole-image-review" class="link-strong">Read the full KJY case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="adequate-views" index="12" eyebrow="Adequate views" title="Grimes v Hawkins & Frimley Park Hospital NHS Foundation Trust" citation="[2011] EWHC 2004 (QB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: failure to complete adequate radiological views before clearing a trauma patient from spinal precautions.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: following spinal injury, initial cervical spine X-rays failed to image down to the C7/T1 junction, missing an unstable C7 fracture. The patient was discharged from spinal immobilisation on inadequate views.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: the Trust was liable for failing to obtain adequate visualisation of the full cervical spine before lifting precautions. This is a breach at the acquisition stage, not an interpretation dispute: no responsible body of opinion would clear the spine on views that do not demonstrate the cervicothoracic junction. MPS guidance (<em>MPS medicolegal.txt</em> extract) lists "failure to request additional views" — including slipped upper femoral epiphysis examples — among the commonest radiology allegations. Modern practice note: where plain films cannot visualise C7/T1, CT is required (cf. RCR major trauma guidance). Same standard in Scotland under <em>Hunter v Hanley</em>.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> trauma clearance is a protocol decision, not an interpretation debate. Grimes makes the cervicothoracic junction the line between a complete and an incomplete examination — clearing on incomplete views is indefensible regardless of what the visible levels showed.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> hard-stop the pathway: no clearance without C7/T1 visualisation, with CT as the default rescue. Document the junction explicitly ("C7/T1 visualised and aligned") so the record proves completeness.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> Grimes is usually won on the images alone — no expert battle on subtle signs is needed if the junction is absent. Check immobilisation records against image coverage and timing.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="triage-delay" index="13" eyebrow="Triage delay" title="Darnley v Croydon Health Services NHS Trust" citation="[2018] UKSC 50">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: breach of duty regarding operational triage, communication of urgency, and delay in arranging critical imaging.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: after a head injury the patient received misleading triage advice about scan wait times, left before an urgent CT, and developed a massive extradural haematoma.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: hospitals owe a duty not to misinform patients in ways that deter them from waiting for urgent, life-saving diagnostic scans. The Supreme Court found breach of duty.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> the scan that is never performed injures as surely as the scan that is misread. Darnley extends the radiology duty beyond the reading room to every staff member whose words determine whether urgent imaging happens.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> agree what front-door staff may say about waits for urgent CT/MRI, audit it, and provide a written escalation route for deteriorating waiters. A radiology service that cannot evidence its triage communications owns the resulting delay.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> Darnley is pleaded as operational breach, not diagnostic error — disclosure should target triage scripts, staffing, and waiting-time records rather than images. It pairs with Shaheen (index 15) on the system-safety theme.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="deferred-mri" index="14" eyebrow="Deferred MRI" title="Jarman v Brighton and Sussex University Hospitals NHS Trust" citation="[2021] EWHC 323 (QB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Appropriateness of triage delay for emergency diagnostic imaging — urgent versus immediate MRI.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/jarman-2021-deferred-mri" class="link-strong">Read the full Jarman case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="unperformed-request" index="15" eyebrow="Unperformed request" title="Shaheen v Daish" citation="[2025] EWHC 3288 (KB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Responsibility for follow-through on imaging requisitions — and whether a patient who never attends contributes negligently to the delay.</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="/recent-developments/shaheen-2025-unperformed-imaging-request" class="link-strong">Read the full Shaheen case commentary in Recent Developments &rarr;</a></p>
</Section>
<Section id="imaging-causation" index="16" eyebrow="Imaging causation" title="C v North Cumbria University Hospitals NHS Trust" citation="[2014] EWHC 61 (QB)">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: medical causation requirements where imaging delays are said to have caused deterioration.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: neurological damage followed a delayed diagnosis in which initial radiological imaging was deferred in favour of clinical observation.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: a claimant must prove on the balance of probabilities that earlier imaging would have altered the definitive outcome, not merely that delay increased the risk of harm. This is orthodox <em>Gregg v Scott [2005] HL</em> territory as summarised in <em>Buchan</em> Ch 7: loss of a chance is not actionable damage in clinical negligence (save the <em>Chester v Afshar [2005] HL</em> consent exception, which does not apply to imaging delay). MDU material (<em>article-1789575458816.pdf</em> extract) confirms causation as a leading defence: claims were discontinued where earlier detection did not alter outcome. Same causation law in Scotland — <em>Gregg</em> binds in both jurisdictions. Scots procedural note: limitation for childhood injury runs from age 16 in Scotland (18 in England and Wales), per the MDU extract.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> breach without causation pays nothing. Most delayed-imaging claims survive or fail on this section, not on the missed finding — the question is always what earlier imaging would have changed, proved with oncology, surgical or neurological evidence.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> when addending a missed finding, record your honest view of whether earlier detection would have altered management — that contemporaneous note often decides causation years later. Avoid overstating ("earlier detection would have cured") or minimising; both will be tested.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> plead causation with a concrete counterfactual — date of earlier diagnosis, stage shift, treatment that would have followed. Loss-of-chance arguments fail outside consent (Gregg); Chester does not rescue an imaging-delay claim.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="catastrophic-quantum" index="17" eyebrow="Catastrophic quantum" title="Sowden v Lodge" citation="[2004] EWCA Civ 1370">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: assessment of long-term care needs and quantum of damages after severe radiological diagnostic failure.</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: severe brain injury resulted from delayed emergency radiological intervention and treatment following trauma.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: leading authority on quantifying future care costs and accommodation needs where imaging errors cause catastrophic neurological injury. <em>Buchan</em> Chs 4 and 27 place Sowden in the damages framework: compensation restores the claimant to the pre-negligence position — principally future care, loss of earnings and accommodation — which is why radiology settlements range from ~£4,000 to ~£2 million with most settled cases above £50,000 and costs often exceeding damages (MDU extract: Achilles-tendon consent case settled at £12,500 damages with costs near five times that). Quantum law is materially the same in Scotland, litigated in the Court of Session under the same heads of damage.</p></div>
<div class="note-box"><p><strong class="strong-hl">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> catastrophic brain injury is where a single missed scan becomes a multi-million-pound claim — care, accommodation and loss of earnings dwarf general damages. Sowden explains why early admission and rehabilitation funding decisions matter as much as liability strategy.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For consultant radiologists:</strong> understand that your addendum feeds a quantum machine: care experts will cost lifelong needs from the injury your delay is said to have caused. Accurate, restrained causal language in reports and duty-of-candour letters constrains that exercise honestly.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="strong-hl">For legal professionals:</strong> use Sowden to structure schedules — future care, accommodation (including adapted housing capital costs), and earnings — and note the MDU cost warning: even modest damages carry costs multiples, so Part 36 and early rehabilitation offers carry unusual weight in imaging cases.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="ai-reporting" index="18" eyebrow="AI-assisted reporting" title="AI-Assisted Reporting: Emerging Medicolegal Risk">
<p class="para-sm-spaced">Issue: who is liable when an AI-assisted report misses or fabricates a finding?</p>
<p class="para-sm">Facts: AI tools are being deployed across NHS radiology services for triage, structured reporting and decision support. The RCR's 3rd edition Standards (October 2025) require that AI-generated content be clearly identified, that a named human author reviews and approves all AI-derived contributions, and that AI report formats must not imply a degree of scrutiny that has not taken place. No published case law directly addresses AI-assisted reporting error at the time of writing.</p>
<div class="rule-quote"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: where an AI tool contributes to a report, the named human author bears full responsibility under Bolam. A radiologist who signs an AI-generated report containing a hallucinated finding without independent verification would fall below standard. An AI tool that silently suppresses a finding through training bias creates a different problem: the report may appear complete but omits a material finding. The employer's duty to provide adequate training (per RCR 2025) and to maintain quality assurance extends to AI-assisted workflows. Precautionary principle: document that an AI tool was used, what it generated, and that you independently verified every clinical finding before release.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="claim-patterns" index="19" eyebrow="Categories" title="Patterns in radiology claims">
<div class="stack-divide-bare"><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">System safety</h3><p class="term-max">Delayed lung cancer on chest radiograph. Breach of fail-safe notification. The duty extends beyond interpretation to effective communication of critical findings. See Shaheen (index 15) and Darnley (index 13).</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">Fracture and spine</h3><p class="term-max">Missed fracture. Cauda equina delay. ED misinterpretation. See FB (index 03), Spellman (index 09), Jarman (index 14) and Grimes (index 12). Standard set by post and task.</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">Interventional error</h3><p class="term-max">Vascular injury. Inadequate consent. Risk disclosure failure. See Montgomery (index 05): the reasonable-patient standard governs material risks for embolisation, biopsy and angioplasty.</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">Abdominal catastrophe</h3><p class="term-max">Missed bowel perforation or ischaemia on CT. Standard of care, causation and protocol adequacy decide breach and outcome. See Brady (index 08) and White (index 10). Causation requires Gregg analysis (index 16).</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">Incidental findings</h3><p class="term-max">37% of abdominal radiology claims cite incidental pathology. Small colonic tumours on unprepared CT. Renal tumours on spine MRI. See Halligan (index 06) and KJY (index 11). The Bolam test, not pure diagnosis, applies.</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">Operational delay</h3><p class="term-max">Misleading triage advice deterring patients from waiting for urgent imaging. Unperformed walk-in referrals. Inadequate views before clearing trauma. See Darnley (index 13), Shaheen (index 15) and Grimes (index 12).</p></div><div class="entry"><h3 class="case-h">AI-assisted error</h3><p class="term-max">AI hallucination, template bias, silent suppression. The named human author bears full responsibility. Document AI use, verify independently.</p></div></div>
</Section>
